  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nTonight: Potential Flurries\n\nAs the main body of an upper trough, churning southeastward \nover northern MN as of 22.18z, rolls over northern WI, cooling \nahead of this in the mid levels will lead to the development of \nshallow surface based instability in central WI. With moisture \navailable around the top of this layer, as evident by the \ncurrently present cumulus field, this may (30%) yield some \nflurries as the top of the boundary layer looks to be below \n-10C. That said, accumulations are highly unlikely (15% per \n22.13z NBM).\n\nThe Week Ahead: Cooler Temperatures\n\nTurn toward lower heights aloft combined with an extended period of \nCAA today and Monday will lead to temperature around and even cooler \nthan normal for the next week. Temperatures Tuesday morning may (25-\n30%) briefly dip into the single digits.\n\nThanksgiving and Friday: Potential Snow in Southwest Wisconsin\n\nWhile cooler temperatures are certain, other details are less so as \nmuch of the eastern CONUS will be under a messy split flow pattern \nstarting Tuesday. Of interest is an upper low that dives southeast \nover the Dakotas and KS/NE before churning eastward and phasing with \na southern stream wave somewhere over the OH/TN Valley. There is some \nchance (20-30%) that far SW WI will be under the wrap around region \nof the resulting upper low with sufficient moisture for snow \nThursday into Friday. These solutions are largely confined to a \ncouple 22.12z CMCE members, 1 22.06z GEFS member, and a few but \nincreasing number of ECENS members. Will need to keep a close eye on \nthis period given potential for outsized road impacts due to timing.",
